WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.133
Before we connect anything, we need some basic
vocabulary for fiber and copper cable.

2
00:00:09.483 --> 00:00:17.070
This video covers what a fiber is, how trunks
break out into legs, the connectors you will

3
00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:23.393
handle, the fiber color code, copper cable,
and how to handle fiber safely.

4
00:00:27.543 --> 00:00:32.513
An optical fiber is a strand of glass about as
thin as a hair.

5
00:00:33.263 --> 00:00:37.231
Equipment sends data through it as pulses of
light.

6
00:00:38.631 --> 00:00:41.106
Fibers usually work in pairs.

7
00:00:41.856 --> 00:00:45.291
One fiber sends, and the other one receives.

8
00:00:46.641 --> 00:00:49.713
In the guide, the letter F means fibers.

9
00:00:50.463 --> 00:00:58.249
So 144F means one hundred forty-four fibers,
and 2F means two fibers.

10
00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:03.376
Fiber carries the huge amounts of AI data.

11
00:01:04.126 --> 00:01:08.435
Copper cable carries the smaller management
connections.

12
00:01:12.585 --> 00:01:19.668
This is the 144-fiber trunk you will install
from every TPU rack.

13
00:01:20.418 --> 00:01:21.997
Look at its shape.

14
00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:29.173
A trunk is a thick cable with many fibers
inside one jacket.

15
00:01:31.473 --> 00:01:34.502
At each end it breaks out at the split point.

16
00:01:35.252 --> 00:01:38.217
Some people call it the furcation point.

17
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:44.303
Each branch is called a leg, and each leg ends
in a connector.

18
00:01:45.053 --> 00:01:47.272
This trunk has eighteen legs.

19
00:01:48.622 --> 00:01:50.969
Each leg carries eight fibers.

20
00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:56.647
Eighteen legs times eight fibers is one
hundred forty-four.

21
00:01:57.397 --> 00:01:59.637
That is where the name comes from.

22
00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:03.035
Sixteen legs are used.

23
00:02:03.785 --> 00:02:06.686
Legs seventeen and eighteen are spares.

24
00:02:10.836 --> 00:02:13.524
You will handle four kinds of connector.

25
00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:16.237
Let us look at each one.

26
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.111
This is an MPO-8 plug.

27
00:02:20.861 --> 00:02:25.597
It is a rectangular push-on plug that holds
eight fibers.

28
00:02:26.347 --> 00:02:33.729
Every leg of the 144 and 192-fiber trunks is
one of these.

29
00:02:36.029 --> 00:02:38.653
This is an LC duplex plug.

30
00:02:39.403 --> 00:02:42.261
It holds two fibers side by side.

31
00:02:43.011 --> 00:02:49.667
The OCS cables use LC plugs with an angled
polish, called APC.

32
00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:53.383
APC plugs are usually green.

33
00:02:55.683 --> 00:03:02.723
Never plug an APC connector into a flat UPC
one, which is usually blue.

34
00:03:03.473 --> 00:03:08.209
The glass does not line up and the end faces
can be damaged.

35
00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:11.753
If they do not match, stop and ask.

36
00:03:13.103 --> 00:03:19.610
This is an RJ45 plug, the square plug on
copper network cable.

37
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.635
And this is a jack, the socket on a copper
panel where a copper run ends.

38
00:03:30.785 --> 00:03:34.347
Legs and fibers are told apart by color.

39
00:03:35.097 --> 00:03:39.471
The colors follow a standard called TIA-598.

40
00:03:40.221 --> 00:03:42.311
You will use it every day.

41
00:03:44.611 --> 00:03:48.985
The first group of four: blue, orange, green,
brown.

42
00:03:50.585 --> 00:03:54.403
The second group: slate, white, red, black.

43
00:03:56.003 --> 00:03:59.929
The third group: yellow, violet, rose, aqua.

44
00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:03.513
Once more, all twelve.

45
00:04:04.263 --> 00:04:06.695
Blue, orange, green, brown.

46
00:04:07.445 --> 00:04:09.685
Slate, white, red, black.

47
00:04:10.435 --> 00:04:13.080
Yellow, violet, rose, aqua.

48
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.760
After twelve, the colors start again.

49
00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:29.879
On the 144-fiber trunk, legs thirteen to
sixteen are blue, orange, green and brown

50
00:04:29.879 --> 00:04:30.491
again.

51
00:04:31.841 --> 00:04:35.191
So a blue leg could be leg one or leg
thirteen.

52
00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:38.522
The color alone cannot tell you.

53
00:04:39.872 --> 00:04:45.099
That is why the guide says: always check the
leg number on the label.

54
00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:48.302
Color helps you find a leg fast.

55
00:04:49.052 --> 00:04:51.079
The number tells you for sure.

56
00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:56.487
Now copper.

57
00:04:57.237 --> 00:05:02.016
CAT6 is copper network cable, also called
Ethernet.

58
00:05:02.766 --> 00:05:06.030
It ends in an RJ45 plug.

59
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:15.431
In this job, copper carries the management and
console connections from each TPU rack to its

60
00:05:15.431 --> 00:05:16.831
end of row rack.

61
00:05:18.181 --> 00:05:20.954
The guide lists copper jacket colors.

62
00:05:21.704 --> 00:05:24.520
Orange is out-of-band management.

63
00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.259
Blue is BMS, the building management system.

64
00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:35.193
Green is FIS, and black is for cables inside
one rack.

65
00:05:36.543 --> 00:05:43.157
The guide says to check which colors your site
stocks, and it does not explain FIS.

66
00:05:43.907 --> 00:05:46.829
If you see green copper, ask your lead.

67
00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:53.305
Last, how to handle fiber.

68
00:05:54.055 --> 00:05:58.535
Rule one: scope and clean every leg before it
goes in.

69
00:05:59.285 --> 00:06:03.850
The scope is a small camera for looking at the
glass end face.

70
00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.067
Why?

71
00:06:07.817 --> 00:06:15.097
Dirt is the number one reason optical networks
need troubleshooting, and it can permanently

72
00:06:15.097 --> 00:06:16.457
damage equipment.

73
00:06:17.207 --> 00:06:19.490
You cannot see it without the scope.

74
00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.549
Rule two: keep the bend wide.

75
00:06:24.299 --> 00:06:32.086
DRS 01.00.01, the cable dressing sheet, sets
the minimum.

76
00:06:32.836 --> 00:06:39.364
While you are pulling, the cable must not bend
tighter than twenty times its own diameter.

77
00:06:40.714 --> 00:06:44.277
With no tension, the minimum is ten times.

78
00:06:45.027 --> 00:06:49.315
If the manufacturer's spec gives a bigger
number, use it.

79
00:06:50.065 --> 00:06:54.502
The guide also recommends fifteen times for
the long term.

80
00:06:55.252 --> 00:06:57.919
Lesson five covers all of this.

81
00:06:59.269 --> 00:07:06.236
For example, a cable a quarter inch thick
needs a five inch bend radius while you pull

82
00:07:06.236 --> 00:07:06.479
it.

83
00:07:07.829 --> 00:07:11.307
And rule three: nothing touches the floor.

84
00:07:12.057 --> 00:07:15.385
Not cables, not slack, not spare legs.

85
00:07:19.535 --> 00:07:22.372
That is the basic vocabulary.

86
00:07:23.122 --> 00:07:29.386
In lesson three of the course, practice
putting the colors in order and making quick

87
00:07:29.386 --> 00:07:31.399
decisions about connectors.

